What This Fixture Asks (SEQ 380 R1, the chain rule)

acF is self-recursive, so the jit builds it as its own function. acH is an ordinary callee, so the jit inlines it into
jit_acF. acH's operand is acF's own argument. The chain rule: an inlined call whose operand is a separately built
function's argument binds to that function's slot, so acH reads the value of THIS activation, not the node the
emit-time binding happened to leave behind.

acF(3) = acH(3) + acH(2) + acH(1) + 0 = 103 + 102 + 101 = 306, depth 4, the guard never tripped. That is the
interpreted twin (same process, after the jitted half) and the intended jitted value.

Before the slot (born red on trunk 2026-10-18): every argument read in jit_acF folds to the emit-time literal 3, so
`argument == 0` never holds and the guard (cap 50) ends the recursion.

H7 for the chain half: with the slot in place, bind the inlined callee to the emit-time node instead of the slot.
acH then reads 3 at every depth while the recursion itself terminates: 103 + 103 + 103 = 309, not 306.
